('128.35.c.6.F',3,'A character slams the door and refuses to speak. A reader can infer the character is:','["delighted", "sleepy", "angry", "hungry"]'::jsonb,2,'Actions suggest feelings.','original'),
('128.35.c.6.F',3,'Evidence that supports an inference comes from:','["the reader''s mood only", "a random guess", "the page number", "words and details in the text"]'::jsonb,3,'Inferences rely on text evidence.','original'),
('128.35.c.6.G',3,'Determining a key idea means deciding which details are:','["most important to the meaning", "at the top of the page", "easiest to spell", "shortest"]'::jsonb,0,'Key ideas carry the main meaning.','original'),
('128.35.c.6.G',3,'In a news article, the key idea is usually supported by:','["the font choice", "important facts and examples", "page numbers", "the date line only"]'::jsonb,1,'Details support the main idea.','original'),
('128.35.c.6.H',3,'Combining a graph and a paragraph to form a conclusion is:','["guessing", "skipping text", "synthesizing information", "copying data"]'::jsonb,2,'Synthesis joins information.','original'),
('128.35.c.6.H',3,'Reading two articles that disagree and forming your own view uses:','["a rhyme scheme", "a guess", "a single source", "synthesis of multiple texts"]'::jsonb,3,'New understanding comes from combining texts.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.A',2,'A reader writes, ''This poem reminds me of my grandfather''s garden.'' This is:','["a personal connection", "a summary", "a citation", "a thesis"]'::jsonb,0,'Personal connections link text to life.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.A',2,'A learner chooses a book on skateboarding because of interest. This is a:','["required text", "self-selected text", "assigned worksheet", "reference table"]'::jsonb,1,'Self-selected reading is chosen by the reader.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.B',3,'A written response should show understanding by:','["drawing a border", "copying the title", "explaining ideas from the text with details", "listing random words"]'::jsonb,2,'Responses demonstrate understanding with details.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.B',3,'When comparing two articles on one topic, a writer should:','["avoid details", "use one only", "list authors only", "note how they agree and differ"]'::jsonb,3,'Comparison needs both similarities and differences.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.C',3,'Text evidence plus original commentary means:','["quoting or citing the text and explaining its meaning", "quoting without explaining", "summarizing without evidence", "giving only an opinion"]'::jsonb,0,'Evidence needs explanation.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.C',3,'In an interpretive response, commentary is used to:','["change the topic", "explain how the evidence supports the idea", "replace the evidence", "fill space"]'::jsonb,1,'Commentary links evidence to the claim.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.D',3,'Which is the best summary of a long story?','["A new ending", "Every detail repeated", "A short statement of the main events in order", "The writer''s opinion only"]'::jsonb,2,'Summaries are brief and ordered.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.D',3,'A paraphrase differs from a quotation because it:','["changes the meaning", "needs no source", "uses the same words", "uses new wording and keeps the meaning"]'::jsonb,3,'Paraphrase restates in new words.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.E',3,'Underlining key words and writing notes in the margin is:','["annotating", "editing", "erasing", "publishing"]'::jsonb,0,'Annotation records thinking.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.E',3,'Freewriting before discussing a text helps a reader to:','["skip the discussion", "gather thoughts without worrying about errors", "correct spelling only", "avoid the text"]'::jsonb,1,'Freewriting generates ideas.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.F',3,'Which sentence uses academic vocabulary?','["The book was good lol.", "The author did stuff.", "The author''s argument is supported by evidence.", "It was cool, I guess."]'::jsonb,2,'Academic language is precise and formal.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.F',3,'Using words learned in a history unit while answering a question shows:','["a rhyme", "a spelling mistake", "a lack of effort", "use of content vocabulary"]'::jsonb,3,'Content words show learning.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.G',3,'''The empty chair sat by the window.'' A reader may discuss the chair''s implicit meaning as:','["a sign of someone''s absence", "a legal notice", "an advertisement", "a table of data"]'::jsonb,0,'Implicit meaning is suggested.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.G',3,'Explicit meaning is the meaning that is:','["hidden", "stated directly", "unspoken", "assumed"]'::jsonb,1,'Explicit means plainly stated.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.H',3,'Which register is best for a letter to a principal?','["texting shorthand", "slangy and rushed", "formal and respectful", "jokey and sarcastic"]'::jsonb,2,'Register should fit the audience.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.H',3,'When speaking to a friend, a learner may use:','["a technical register", "a ceremonial register", "a legal register", "a casual register"]'::jsonb,3,'Casual language suits friends.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.I',3,'After hearing a classmate''s strong evidence, a reader changes her interpretation. This shows:','["willingness to reflect and adjust", "copying", "lack of effort", "poor reading"]'::jsonb,0,'Good readers revise ideas.','original'),
('128.35.c.7.I',3,'Valid evidence is evidence that is:','["long", "accurate and relevant", "popular only", "loud"]'::jsonb,1,'Valid evidence is sound and related to the claim.','original'),
